WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.780
What's up? Welcome to Models and Agents episode 52 from May 18th, 2026.

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:09.340
New week in AI, and if last week was anything to go by, buckle up.

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:13.260
Let's get into it. In video's 4-bit pre-training technique,

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:19.020
cuts memory needs for large hybrid models while keeping accuracy nearly identical to FP8.

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:24.900
The core development is a full 4-bit pre-training stack called NVFP4.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:29.700
It was validated on a 12-billion-parameter Mamba transformer hybrid

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:36.880
trained for 10 trillion tokens. The method combines selective BF-16 layers for critical weights,

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:43.060
16 x 16 random Hadamard transforms on ingredients, two-dimensional weight scaling,

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:50.900
and stochastic rounding. Downstream accuracy stayed within 0.04 points of the FP8 baseline.

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:58.340
That is 62.58% versus 62.62% on MMLU Pro.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:04.720
Teams running extended pre-training on constrained GPU fleets now have a documented path

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:10.440
to reduce memory and interconnect pressure. Watch for follow-up work on larger dense models

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:14.560
and whether the same recipe transfers to pure transformer architectures.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:20.920
Community forecasts point to new open-weight model drops, likely landing between late May and early

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:28.760
June. Postures on R-slash Local L-L-L-A-M-A notice shift in how open-weight submissions are being handled.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:33.160
They are watching for signs that major labs are extending the current cadence.

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:42.600
Tests on 23.535 BA3B and its no refusal fine-tune show register dependent expert routing

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:48.780
even before refusal layers activate. AAV-E coded safety prompts produce different first token

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:53.740
routing tensors and longer thinking traces than matched academic English prompts.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:59.200
This raises questions about whether refusal alone masks underlying safety gaps.

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:07.600
Luce D-flash plus P-flash delivered 2.24 times decode and 3.05 times pre-fills speed-ups

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:16.940
versus Lama dot CPP hip on an RX-7900 XTX. The optimal DDTree budget was 8 rather than the 22

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:23.780
recommended for Strix Halo. This highlights how GDDR6 bandwidth changes the best speculation strategy.

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:31.640
A new agent built specifically for 4B to 14B local models reaches 87% on a coding benchmarks.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:37.460
It replaces chain tool calls with compound actions, automatic lint and fixed loops,

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:45.200
and on-demand escalation to larger models. Small code runs against LM Studio or Lama endpoints

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:53.100
and keeps 95% of work local. Developers are exploring dedicated local models that read a testing.md

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:59.580
or quality.md file. These models continuously refactor generated code for security,

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:04.680
readability and maintainability, without sending large code bases to cloud APIs.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:13.340
Simon Willison argues that useful AI-generated tools save reviewers time, while unedited LLM

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:19.600
slop wastes it. He recommends including detailed terminal sessions that demonstrate exactly what

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:26.400
was tried and how success was verified. Any LLM output containing opinions or anecdotes must be

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:32.340
closely directed and reviewed by a human. Willison calls out examples where models falsely claim

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:39.660
months of personal usage as particularly harmful. Carpathi noted that simply saying boot in 8 times

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:47.220
H100 in a tutorial immediately blocks most viewers at step 1. This highlights the gap between assumed

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:53.860
hardware access and real-world availability. Okay, let's pop the hood on data mixing. Everyone

00:03:53.860 --> 00:04:00.160
talks about data mixing as a one-time hyperparameter you set at the start of training. In practice,

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:05.520
it is a recurring online decision that must be solved at every phase of the model lifecycle.

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:11.080
The core insight is that you can cheaply simulate candidate mixtures by interpolating between

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:16.780
low-rank adapters already trained on the current checkpoint. This avoids training separate proxy

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:22.280
models from scratch. The approach keeps the surge grounded in the model's actual learning dynamics

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:28.560
rather than an earlier snapshot. It adds negligible overhead per decision yet finds mixtures that

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:34.120
improve average perplexity by roughly 6% over static baselines during pre-training.

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:41.440
In continual learning it matches full retraining or distillation performance while using 66-95%

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:47.780
less compute. The practical takeaway is to treat mixing as a lightweight inner loop that runs every

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:53.760
few hundred steps rather than a single upfront choice. The main gotcha is forgetting to re-evaluate

00:04:53.760 --> 00:05:00.300
when the data distribution or training objective shifts. Test the loose D-flash plus P-flash set up

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:09.120
on your AMD card with 2.3.627b. See whether a budget equals 8 gives you the reported 2.24 times speed

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:17.200
up on short generations. Run small code against a local 4B to 7B model on a real code base. Compare failure

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:22.160
rates to your current agent workflow. Add a short terminal session block to your next project

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:28.940
readme to show exactly how you verified functionality. Follow Simon Willison's recommendation on that one.

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:35.560
Experiment with op-mix style low-rank adapter interpolation on your next continued pre-training run.

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:39.840
This keeps data mixing decisions current without extra proxy models.

